{"content":[{"c":["RAMAH"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["1. One of the cities located in the territory allotted to Benjamin’s tribe for an inheritance, listed between Gibeon and Beeroth (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.18.25","r":"Josh.18.25","rawR":"Josh.18.25","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.18.25","value":"Josh.18.25"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 18:25"},"). In the proximity of this town, Rachel, Jacob’s wife, was buried (",{"href":"?bref=Matt.2.18","r":"Matt.2.18","rawR":"Matt.2.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.2.18","value":"Matt.2.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mt 2:18"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.35.16-21","r":"Gen.35.16-21","rawR":"Gen.35.16-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.35.16-21","value":"Gen.35.16-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 35:16-21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.31.15","r":"Jer.31.15","rawR":"Jer.31.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.31.15","value":"Jer.31.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 31:15"},"). Ramah, positioned near Bethel, was the place where Deborah judged Israel (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.4.5","r":"Judg.4.5","rawR":"Judg.4.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.4.5","value":"Judg.4.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 4:5"},"). This city was a temporary resting place for a Levite and his concubine traveling north from Bethlehem (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.19.13","r":"Judg.19.13","rawR":"Judg.19.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.19.13","value":"Judg.19.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:13"},")."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["During the period of the divided kingdom (930– 722 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"), King Baasha of Israel (908–886 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},") fortified Ramah. From Ramah, Baasha was able to prevent an invasion of King Asa’s (910–869 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},") Judean army. Baasha later abandoned the city and hurried his army north to repulse a Syrian offensive led by King Ben-hadad I (c. 885 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"). Asa dismantled Ramah’s military fortifications, using the material to build the towns of Geba and Mizpah (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.15.17-22","r":"1Kgs.15.17-22","rawR":"1Kgs.15.17-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.15.17-22","value":"1Kgs.15.17-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 15:17-22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.16.1-6","r":"2Chr.16.1-6","rawR":"2Chr.16.1-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.16.1-6","value":"2Chr.16.1-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 16:1-6"},")."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["The cities of Geba, Ramah, and Gibeah formed the route taken by the Assyrian army during Sennacherib’s military incursion into Judah (c. 701 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},") against King Hezekiah and Jerusalem (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.10.29","r":"Isa.10.29","rawR":"Isa.10.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.10.29","value":"Isa.10.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 10:29"},"). Later King Nebuchadnezzar used Ramah as a place of detainment for the Jews being deported to Babylon. Here Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guard, released Jeremiah from among the captives (",{"href":"?bref=Jer.40.1","r":"Jer.40.1","rawR":"Jer.40.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.40.1","value":"Jer.40.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 40:1"},")."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["Following the Babylonian captivity, inhabitants of Ramah returned with Zerubbabel to Palestine and rebuilt this city (",{"href":"?bref=Ezra.2.26","r":"Ezra.2.26","rawR":"Ezra.2.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezra.2.26","value":"Ezra.2.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezr 2:26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.7.30","r":"Neh.7.30","rawR":"Neh.7.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.7.30","value":"Neh.7.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"Neh 7:30"},"). Some suggest that the postexilic town of Ramah was another Benjaminite town located farther west near the coastal plain (",{"href":"?bref=Neh.11.33","r":"Neh.11.33","rawR":"Neh.11.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.11.33","value":"Neh.11.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Neh 11:33"},"). The site of Ramah is identified with the modern village of er-Ram, five miles (8 kilometers) north of Jerusalem."],"sourceClass":"list-text","t":"p"},{"c":["2. City in the Negev marking the southern extremity of the territory allotted to the tribe of Simeon within Judah’s inheritance (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.19.8","r":"Josh.19.8","rawR":"Josh.19.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.19.8","value":"Josh.19.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 19:8"},"); also called Ramoth of the Negev (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.30.27","r":"1Sam.30.27","rawR":"1Sam.30.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.30.27","value":"1Sam.30.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 30:27"},") and Baalath-beer (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.19.8","r":"Josh.19.8","rawR":"Josh.19.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.19.8","value":"Josh.19.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 19:8"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.4.33","r":"1Chr.4.33","rawR":"1Chr.4.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.4.33","value":"1Chr.4.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 4:33"},"). ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=BaalathBeer_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"BaalathBeer_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"BaalathBeer_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Baalath-beer"},"."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["3. Town defining the boundary of the territory assigned to Asher’s tribe for an inheritance, mentioned between Sidon and Tyre (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.19.29","r":"Josh.19.29","rawR":"Josh.19.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.19.29","value":"Josh.19.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 19:29"},")."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["4. One of 19 fortified cities given to Naphtali’s tribe for a possession, mentioned between Adamah and Hazor (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.19.36","r":"Josh.19.36","rawR":"Josh.19.36","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.19.36","value":"Josh.19.36"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 19:36"},"). Its location is identifiable with the modern town of er-Rameh, about 11 miles (17.7 kilometers) northwest of the Sea of Galilee."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["5. Home of Samuel’s parents, Elkanah and Hannah; the birthplace of Samuel (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.1.19","r":"1Sam.1.19","rawR":"1Sam.1.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.1.19","value":"1Sam.1.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 1:19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.2.11","r":"1Sam.2.11","rawR":"1Sam.2.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.2.11","value":"1Sam.2.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:11"},"); and later his home (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.7.17","r":"1Sam.7.17","rawR":"1Sam.7.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.7.17","value":"1Sam.7.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.16.13","r":"1Sam.16.13","rawR":"1Sam.16.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.16.13","value":"1Sam.16.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"16:13"},"). Samuel judged Israel from Ramah, Bethel, Gilgal, and Mizpah (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.7.17","r":"1Sam.7.17","rawR":"1Sam.7.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.7.17","value":"1Sam.7.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:17"},"). Saul first met Samuel at this city (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.9.6-10","r":"1Sam.9.6-10","rawR":"1Sam.9.6-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.9.6-10","value":"1Sam.9.6-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:6-10"},"). Here the elders of Israel petitioned Samuel to appoint a king for them (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.8.4","r":"1Sam.8.4","rawR":"1Sam.8.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.8.4","value":"1Sam.8.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"8:4"},"), and later this city provided David a place of refuge from King Saul (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.19.18-20.1","r":"1Sam.19.18-20.1","rawR":"1Sam.19.18-20.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.19.18-20.1","value":"1Sam.19.18-20.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:18–20:1"},"). Samuel was buried at Ramah (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.25.1","r":"1Sam.25.1","rawR":"1Sam.25.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.25.1","value":"1Sam.25.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"25:1"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.28.3","r":"1Sam.28.3","rawR":"1Sam.28.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.28.3","value":"1Sam.28.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"28:3"},"). Ramah is alternately called Ramathaim-zophim in ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.1.1","r":"1Sam.1.1","rawR":"1Sam.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.1.1","value":"1Sam.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Samuel 1:1"},"."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"},{"c":["6. Abbreviated name for Ramoth-gilead (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.8.29","r":"2Kgs.8.29","rawR":"2Kgs.8.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.8.29","value":"2Kgs.8.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 8:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.22.6","r":"2Chr.22.6","rawR":"2Chr.22.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.22.6","value":"2Chr.22.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 22:6"},"). ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=RamothGilead_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"RamothGilead_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"RamothGilead_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Ramoth-gilead"},"."],"sourceClass":"list","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Ramah","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jos 18:25","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Josh.18.25","rawQueryValue":"Josh.18.25","sourceRefExact":"Josh.18.25"},{"anchorText":"Mt 2:18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Matt.2.18","rawQueryValue":"Matt.2.18","sourceRefExact":"Matt.2.18"},{"anchorText":"Gn 35:16-21","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.35.16-21","rawQueryValue":"Gen.35.16-21","sourceRefExact":"Gen.35.16-21"},{"anchorText":"Jer 31:15","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Jer.31.15","rawQueryValue":"Jer.31.15","sourceRefExact":"Jer.31.15"},{"anchorText":"Jgs 4:5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.4.5","rawQueryValue":"Judg.4.5","sourceRefExact":"Judg.4.5"},{"anchorText":"19:13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.19.13","rawQueryValue":"Judg.19.13","sourceRefExact":"Judg.19.13"},{"anchorText":"1 Kgs 15:17-22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Kgs.15.17-22","rawQueryValue":"1Kgs.15.17-22","sourceRefExact":"1Kgs.15.17-22"},{"anchorText":"2 Chr 16:1-6","canonic